  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Bembe\"\nsubtitle: \"Democratic Republic of the Congo\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Bembe people, also called Babembe, are a Bantu people. |\n| Where | Eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo and western Kigoma Region of Tanzania, mainly around Fizi, Tanganyika, and the Itombwe Mountains. |\n| How many | About 252,000 in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 1991; around 1.5 million in 2005. |\n| Language | Kibembe. |\n| Religion | The profile mentions Christian missions and Arab-Swahili Muslim customs, but does not give a full account of current religion. |\n| Known for | Mountain farming · Hunting and fishing · Drums and dance · Cassava foods · Named wooden, metal, ceramic, and textile objects |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Bembe live west of Lake Tanganyika in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo and western Tanzania. They speak Kibembe and are related to other Bantu-speaking peoples. Their documented culture includes mountain farming, hunting, fishing, drums, dance, cassava foods, and many named objects. Oral traditions trace the Babembe to a common ancestor named Mbondo.\n\n## Material culture\n\nBembe material culture includes named wooden, metal, ceramic, textile, and woven objects, but the sources give little detail about buildings, clothing, and textile patterns.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nThe sources name textile reliquary figures but do not describe Bembe weaving or patterns.\n\n- **Textile reliquary figure** (*muzidi*) — A muzidi is generally seated, with a long narrow body and extended limbs ending in clearly shaped fingers and toes.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe sources mention a straw hat and rings but do not describe everyday or ceremonial dress.\n\n- **Straw hat** (*mpu yangara*) — The mpu yangara is a brimmed, Europeanized straw hat associated with Babembe.\n- **Neck ring** — A neck ring was worn by both sexes.\n- **Iron head or neck ring** — An iron head or neck ring is associated with Babembe-bambamba.\n\n### Architecture\n\nThe sources do not describe Bembe houses, settlements, or named building types.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nBembe objects include knives, axes, pots, bowls, pipes, bells, baskets, and other tools made from wood, metal, clay, hide, and bark.\n\n- **Knife** (*kwiti*) — Kwiti is a named Bembe knife form.\n- **Knife** (*mbesi*) — Mbesi is another named Bembe knife form.\n- **Axe** (*pukweto*) — Pukweto is a named Bembe axe form.\n- **Clay pot** (*Bisa*) — Bisa is a named Bembe clay pot.\n- **Wooden bell** (*madibu ma banganga*) — Madibu ma banganga are wooden bells with three clappers.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nBembe adornment includes iron neck rings and yellow-metal rings for the arms or feet.\n\n- **Looped iron neck ring** — Some iron neck rings have loops containing pieces of yellow metal.\n- **Opening iron ring** — Some iron rings can be opened with a simple hand movement.\n- **Arm or foot ring** — Yellow-metal arm or foot rings were recorded, including one associated with Bakuta smiths and export to Basundi-babembe.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nBembe folk music uses drums and is played in celebration, with a six-beat style and links to newer genres.\n\n- **Drum music** — Bembe folk music traditionally uses drums, and the way the drum is played can be called a six-beat tune.\n- **Large wooden bells** (*madibu ma banganga*) — Madibu ma banganga are large wooden bells with three clappers.\n- **Women’s pipe** (*Kipi*) — Kipi is a named women’s pipe.\n- **Wooden powder horn** (*Mpipi*) — Mpipi is a named wooden powder horn.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nThe Bembe folk dance Esuba uses shoulder and waist movements and goes with drum music.\n\n- **Folk dance** (*Esuba*) — Esuba uses movements of the shoulder and waist and accompanies folk music played on drums.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe sources do not name festivals or life-cycle rites; they only say that music and dance are performed in celebration.\n\n- **Celebratory music and dance** — Bembe music and its accompanying dance are performed in celebration of any sort.\n\n## Foodways\n\nBabembe cuisine centers on cassava foods, fish, and vegetable stews.\n\n- **Cassava dish** (*Kyenge*) — Kyenge is a cassava food compared in the source to fufu, and the Wabembe are especially fond of it.\n- **Cassava dish** (*eswaka*) — Eswaka is a cassava food compared in the source to fufu.\n- **Cassava-leaf stew** (*sombe*) — Sombe is made from pounded cassava leaves, preferably with ground nuts and palm oil.\n- **Small fish** (*Dagaa*) — Dagaa is described as a very small fish.\n- **Fish** (*Mkeke*) — Mkeke, also written Mkebuka, is a named fish eaten by the Babembe.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nTraditional griots preserve genealogies and stories about peace and the origins of the Babembe.\n\n- **Common ancestor** (*Mbondo*) — Genealogical accounts trace the Babembe to a common ancestor named Mbondo, also written M'mbondo.\n- **Peace tradition** (*Kalembelembe*) — The name Kalembelembe is linked to the declaration “Tunapenda Kalembe,” translated as “We love peace,” and signified “abundant peace.”\n\n## Language & religion\n\nBembe is a Bantu language called Kibembe, while the profile mentions Christian missions and Arab-Swahili Muslim customs.\n\n- **Bembe language** (*Kibembe*) — Kibembe is the Bantu language spoken by the Bembe in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and western Tanzania.\n- **Language prefix** (*Ki*) — Regional languages were classified by adding the noun-class marker Ki to place-name roots, giving Kibembe for the language of the Bembe.\n- **Christian missions** — Christian missions, catechism, adult baptisms, and campaigns against slavery are described at Kibanga and Mpala.\n- **Arab-Swahili Muslim customs** — The historical material mentions Arab-Swahili Muslim customs among Wanguana.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *muzidi* — textile reliquary figure\n- *mpu yangara* — brimmed straw hat\n- *kwiti* — knife form\n- *mbesi* — knife form\n- *pukweto* — axe form\n- *Bisa* — clay pot\n- *Mpipi* — wooden powder horn\n- *Kipi* — women’s pipe\n- *madibu ma banganga* — wooden bells\n- *Esuba* — Bembe folk dance\n- *Mkeke* — fish\n- *Mkebuka* — another name for Mkeke\n- *Dagaa* — very small fish\n- *Kyenge* — cassava food\n- *eswaka* — cassava food\n- *sombe* — cassava-leaf stew\n- *Mbondo* — common ancestor in genealogy\n- *M'mbondo* — another spelling of Mbondo\n- *Kalembelembe* — name linked with abundant peace\n- *Kibembe* — Bembe language\n- *Ki* — noun-class marker\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- “Bembe people,” Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bembe_people\n- “Bembe language,” Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bembe_language\n- Museum catalogue records supplied for British Museum, Museum of World Culture, and Museum of Ethnography objects associated with Babembe or Bembe.\n- The UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage sources supplied contain no inscriptions for this ethnic group or for the Democratic Republic of the Congo.\n\n",
